<details class="methodology" id="methodology">
	<summary>
		<div>
			<div class="section-number">A NOTE ON THE NUMBERS</div>
			<h2>
				Useful perspective,
				<br />
				<em>not a precise footprint.</em>
			</h2>
		</div>
	</summary>
	<div class="method-copy">
		<p>
			We multiply your prompt count by a published reference: ChatGPT's reported
			average query (0.34 Wh and 0.000085 US gallons of water), Google's median
			Gemini Apps text prompt (0.24 Wh and 0.26 mL), Mistral Large 2's 400-token
			response (45 mL of water), BLOOM's average query (4 Wh), or a LLaMA 65B
			benchmark response (approximately 0.3 Wh). The BLOOM and LLaMA figures are
			reported in Google's research summary. Estimates with no water or energy
			figure leave those results unavailable.
		</p>
		<p>
			These studies use different methods and boundaries and are not directly
			comparable. BLOOM was measured without batching; LLaMA was benchmarked on
			specific hardware. Mistral's water figure includes upstream impacts. Long
			prompts, reasoning, images, model choice, and infrastructure can change
			the result substantially.
		</p>
		<p>
			Carbon is an illustrative proxy, not actual emissions: energy is
			multiplied by the US EPA's 2022 average electricity factor of 394 g
			CO₂/kWh. Driving uses the EPA's 393 g CO₂e per mile for an average US
			gasoline vehicle. Tree-days use its approximately 60.5 kg CO₂ per urban
			tree per year averaged over 10 years; trees vary and do not instantly
			offset emissions. The 10 W bulb and 250 mL cup are illustrative.
		</p>
		<p>
			Food comparisons divide the carbon proxy by approximate food greenhouse
			gas emissions. Bread assumes a 40 g slice of conventional baguette at 0.56
			kg CO₂e per kg; beer assumes 330 mL at 0.771 kg CO₂e per litre. Both come
			from one cradle-to-gate study, not global averages. Beef burger means only
			a 100 g beef patty, not the bun, toppings or cooking, using the global
			average of 99.48 kg CO₂e per kg of beef from beef herds. Serving sizes are
			illustrative. Food CO₂e includes other greenhouse gases and has broader
			boundaries than the electricity-only CO₂ proxy, so these are rough
			comparisons, not measurements or offsets.
		</p>
		<p>
			Results are not a full lifecycle assessment: training and other impacts
			are not included. Water estimates have different boundaries; Google's
			figure covers data-centre cooling, Mistral includes upstream impacts, and
			OpenAI's disclosure does not explain its methodology. Mistral also reports
			lifecycle emissions, but those are not used in the electricity-based
			carbon proxy shown here.
		</p>
